            <div xml:id="d569">

                <pb facs="0836" n="822" sameAs="frus:frus1946v06/pg822"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/1–2246</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chairman of the Government Purchasing Commission
                        of the Soviet Union in the U.S.A</hi>. (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Rudenko</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to Mr. Willard L. Thorp, Deputy to the
                        Assistant Secretary of State for Economic Affairs</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-01-22">January 22, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Thorp</hi>: With reference to your letter of
                    January 4, 1946, I wish to state that the question raised in your letter is
                    beyond the competence of the Government Purchasing Commission of the Soviet
                    Union in the U.S.A.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">L. Rudenko</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
